A||||9||deceler8||Lower ball joints...Any helpful hints ?||||5||
Z||000000||deceler8||06-04-2000||06:48 PM||||Replaced the uppers...pretty simple...old ones were crap. Now thinking about lowers.<P>I have the ball joints and the press...the Chilton manual is worthless and I want to know how to best do this from someone who has done it...mine is an '86 2m4. <P>What is the easiest way to go about this...what do I need and what do I have to be careful of ? How much of the front end do I have to take apart and do I have to mess with the spring ?<P>I really appreciate any help any of you can provide. Just trying to keep the shiny side up (LOL).||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||06-04-2000||07:20 PM||||Ok, Once the wheels are off, jack up the lower control arms a little and support them with jack stands or blocks, in other words, don't let them just hang down by themselves. Sometimes the original lower ball joints might have a spot weld or two that will need to be chiseled off. If your press is one of those little ball joint presses that is the correct size, then it should be easy. Remove the cotter pin and nut, pop it off the spindle with a pickle fork, then press it out. Usually it don't go that easy though. Lots of times you have to remove the whole lower control arm and take it to a shop with a hydraulic press, that's what I had to do. If you do take the lower control arm off, that's when you have to deal with the springs. One way is to run a chain through it so they won't pop out, but with a Fiero, that's not really needed if you're careful. If the front end is high enough, the lower control arm will drop down (do this slowly) far enough to remove the spring. If the spring sticks to the upper rubber spacer, then don't worry about it. It doesn't have to come out unless you're replacing springs too. Of course you also have to remove the sway bar end links too. Usually the hardest part of the control arm removal is getting the blasted bolts out of the bushings. Good luck.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||06-04-2000||07:24 PM||||Oh yeah, there's a little pocket on the lower control arm that the end of the coil spring fits into. Make sure that all lines up right before you get it all back together.||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowCG||06-04-2000||07:48 PM||||Maybe some heat would help to expand the control arm a bit?<BR>Or some good penetrating oil to break to rust bond?||||reg||1
Z||000004||flexableface||06-04-2000||09:56 PM||||i just got done replacing the lower ball joints along with a few other thing in the front end. what i did to get the lower ball joints out was to place a 4x4 board under the control arm and took a large hammer and beat the the hell out of the ball joint and using a litle heat will help, especially when you are putting the new ball joints back in. Whats also helps it to put a little chamfer on the spline on the ball joint which helps it go in easier. good luck<BR>      luke simpson||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||06-04-2000||10:27 PM||||Beating on the ball joints is a good way to tweak your control arm and/or ruin the new ball joints, not recommended. Pressing them out is the best.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||06-04-2000||10:44 PM||||I just took mine to a shop. 30 minutes later (and $30) They had old ones out and new ones in. (and they pressed in my poly bushings too!)||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phoenix||06-05-2000||08:53 AM||||I just replaced my lower ball joints on my 88 this past Saturday.  I removed the lower a-arm (fairly easy) and pressed the joints out using a bench-top 6 ton press.  Even with the press they were a bugger to get out!  Pressed the new ones in and reassebled every thing. A straight forward job, took about 4 hours.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||06-05-2000||09:40 AM||||Just out of curiousity (well, maybe more as I plan to replace my springs in a few months), are the rear springs as easy to replace?||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||06-05-2000||11:51 AM||||Of course the rear springs are attached to the struts. Fiero rear struts are easy to drop out, but you usually mess up the rear alignment, so you might as well replace the struts while you're at it too. Once the whole spring/strut assemblies are out, you need to buy, beg, borrow, or steal a spring compresser (many major auto parts stores have a tool loaner program). Once it's all back together, take it to a shop for an alignment. If you drive it much before that, you'll toast the rear tires fairly quickly.||||reg||1
